   "title": "Boeing, 747-400 Airplane Characteristics for Airport Planning (D6-58326-1 Rev F, December 2024), 2.2.1 General Dimensions: Model 747-400",
   "accessed": "2026-11-25",
   "note": "The nose, cockpit, fuselage, main-deck windows, doors, wing, nacelles, fin and tailplane are the 747-400's measured outlines (shapes/B744-passenger.json; the upper deck is the 747-400F's short one, shapes/B744.json), changed by tools/trace/b742/derive.py as D6-58326's 747-100B/-200 pages say."
  },
  {
   "url": "https://www.boeing.com/content/dam/boeing/v2/airports/acaps/747_123sp.pdf",
   "title": "Boeing, 747 Airplane Characteristics for Airport Planning, Models 747-100, -200, -300, SP (D6-58326, May 1984, the 2023 reissue on boeing.com): 2.2.1 General Dimensions, Models 747-100B, -200, -300; 2.3.1 Ground Clearances; 2.7.1 Main Entry Door Locations; 2.7.6 Upper Deck Exit Door",
   "accessed": "2026-11-25",
   "note": "A scan: 2.2.1 is drawn in lines about 0.35 m thick on a 5 ft grid, too coarse to trace. Its figures (225 ft 2 in to the tail cone, 231 ft 10 in overall, printed 70.4 m but 70.66 m in feet, 229 ft 2 in to the tailplane's tips, 195 ft 8 in span, 72 ft 9 in tailplane, 21 ft 4 in width) are the 747-400's but the span, which is cut to it. Its plan prints the engines 39 ft 9 in and 69 ft 10 in out (12.1 m, 21.3 m); its front view draws their inlets 11.9 m and 21.1 m out, where the 747-400's are kept (11.97 m, 21.18 m). 2.3.1 puts the fin's tip 9.64-10.15 m above the aft crown (K less H, minimum and maximum: two attitudes); the 747-400's fin is 10.08 m, kept. 2.7.1's doors (9.50, 18.80, 30.61, 40.74, 55.14 m) are the 747-400's. The upper deck exit door is 2.7.6's (0.61 by 1.22 m at 8.00 m). The JT9D-7 nacelles aren't told apart from the 747-400's RB211s at the scan's resolution, so the 747-400's stand in for them. Confidence medium."
  },
  {
   "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Boeing_747-131,_Trans_World_Airlines_(TWA)_JP5896855.jpg",
